---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
import '../../../styles/docs-prose.css';
---

<Layout title="Strokes — Varve Docs" description="How to edit, transfer, render, and export strokes in Varve.">
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Tools / Strokes
      </nav>

      <h1 class="site-page-title">Strokes</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        A stroke is an ordered, editable outline attached to a shape, path, text, frame, table, or image-bearing shape. It owns its paint and geometry settings and remains separate from raster brush marks, selection outlines, and guides.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Raster brush marks</h2>
        <p>
          This guide covers editable vector strokes. For pixel-level illustration work, use the existing Paint, Eraser, and Smudge tools on the same canvas and document. Smudge exposes three reservoir modes in Tool options: <strong>Pure smudge</strong>, <strong>Loaded paint</strong>, and <strong>Fingerpaint</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Sample merged layers</strong> is an explicit opt-in setting. It reads a read-only snapshot of visible raster-layer tiles while depositing only on the active target, which makes clipped texture and paintover passes safer. It does not implicitly sample vector nodes, group isolation, effects, or transformed non-raster content; use an explicit flattened raster copy when those sources are required.
        </p>
        <h3>Selections into flats</h3>
        <ol>
          <li>Create a marquee, pixel-lasso, quick-mask, or image-derived selection.</li>
          <li>Select one visible, unlocked pixel layer as the destination.</li>
          <li>Open <strong>Selection Sources</strong> in the Inspector and choose <strong>Fill pixel layer</strong>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The fill is one undoable tile mutation and leaves the selection active for another colour pass. Feathered or anti-aliased selection coverage remains soft; a locked, hidden, vector, or image-only target is refused instead of being silently rasterized. This is a selection fill, not a full line-art bucket detector: use the existing image-derived Magic Wand or lasso to define the region first.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Inspector workflow</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Select a supported layer and open <strong>Stroke</strong> in the Inspector.</li>
          <li>Set paint, width, visibility, and alignment in the compact row.</li>
          <li>The collapsed <strong>Advanced</strong> toggle names every non-default setting ("Advanced · Dashed · Per-side"), so a customised stroke never reads as plain.</li>
          <li>Open <strong>Advanced</strong> for dash presets, caps, joins, per-side rectangle widths, endpoint markers, gradient paint, and dash phase. Controls that do not apply are omitted rather than disabled: miter limit appears only for miter joins, dash phase only while dashed, arrowheads only for lines and open paths, and per-side widths behind a switch that offers <strong>Use one width</strong> to return to a uniform outline.</li>
          <li>Use the up/down controls to change stroke paint order, or the direct controls on stacked strokes. Reordering keeps each stroke instance's identity.</li>
          <li><strong>Add Stroke</strong> copies the layer's previous stroke — colour, width, dash, and caps — so a repeated outline does not restart from the 1px black default.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Dash style offers Solid, Dashed, Dotted, and Dash-dot presets in absolute canvas units. Choose <strong>Custom</strong> to enter an exact comma- or space-separated pattern; malformed values are rejected instead of partially applied. A zero-width stroke is flagged as invisible on its row.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Alignment and geometry</h2>
        <p>Center strokes straddle the authored path. Inside and outside alignment are defined against an enclosed region and fill rule, not against whether a fill happens to be opaque. Open paths and lines keep centered behavior because “inside” and “outside” have no unambiguous region meaning there.</p>
        <p>Closed Bézier paths retain their final curved segment. Compound path contours and holes are included in the outline and use the path fill rule.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Layer and runtime support</h2>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>Live Canvas2D / WASM IR</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Shape and path</td><td>Full live stroke replay</td><td>Center/inside/outside for regions, caps, joins, dashes, gradients, pressure profiles, markers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Editable text</td><td>Glyph stroke in Canvas2D</td><td>Text remains editable; text-on-path uses the existing path layout route</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Image-bearing shape</td><td>Frame border and alpha-aware stroke paths</td><td>Does not silently turn an image border into a subject silhouette</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Frame/table</td><td>Supported in live replay</td><td>Export support depends on the target and may rasterize the boundary</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Native desktop</td><td>Same camelCase wire fields</td><td>Native scene mirror preserves stroke identity, alignment, per-side weights, markers, and gradients</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Export behavior</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>PNG/raster:</strong> uses the full Canvas2D replay path.</li>
          <li><strong>SVG:</strong> supported center strokes, spatial linear/radial gradients, dashes, caps/joins/miter limits, endpoint markers, and editable text strokes remain native. Unsupported alignment, per-side, pressure-variable, image-border, frame, or complex stack cases use a local raster fallback.</li>
          <li><strong>PDF/print:</strong> native path output covers the basic center-stroke contract; unsupported stroke features are routed through the export compositor so a PDF does not silently lose them.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Raster fallback is bounded to the smallest affected export boundary. It preserves the live appearance while keeping unrelated siblings vector-editable where the format allows it.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>History and transfer</h2>
        <p>Stroke edits use the existing editor transaction coordinator. Copy/paste and style transfer clone the stroke payload and mint independent destination IDs, so later edits do not mutate the source or collide with its bindings. Legacy documents without IDs receive deterministic IDs at codec normalization.</p>
      </section>

      <div class="docs-cta">
        <h2>Related guides</h2>
        <p>See <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/vector')}>Vector Tools</a>, <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/typography')}>Typography</a>, and <a href={sitePath('/features/export')}>Export &amp; Code Generation</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0; }
  .docs-section h2 { border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-accent); padding-bottom: var(--space-3); }

  .docs-section table { display: block; max-width: 100%; overflow-x: auto; }

  .docs-section th { background: var(--surface-muted); }

</style>
